  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on self-reported, recalled experiences of a single recent visit rather than real-time or objective physiological measurements of restoration.",
      "what": "Using survey data from 17,131 participants across 19 countries, this study analyzed how 17 different types of blue spaces—ranging from marine to inland standing and flowing waters—aided psychological restoration and nature connectedness. Linear mixed models and path analysis revealed that sandy beaches and rural rivers or canals were recalled as the most restorative, while marshlands were the least. Perceived safety emerged as the strongest quality indicator for positive outcomes, and active water engagement like swimming yielded higher restoration than staying on land, with benefits peaking at around 3 hours.",
      "why": "It provides multi-country empirical evidence establishing how specific physical characteristics, safety perceptions, and activity types within blue spaces directly influence cognitive recovery and human-nature connection, moving beyond generic green-space assumptions."
    }
  },
